    (3, None, '\'Step aside a little, do not disturb me!\' Barinov gives two blunt "ty" commands. "Otoydi" starts with "ot-", away, and "ne meshay mne" is literally "don\'t hinder to me": the verb "meshat\'" takes "mne", "to me", where English just says "me".'),
    (4, None, '\'Excuse me, Chef, I am simply watching carefully.\' "Izvinite" is the formal "excuse me", and "smotryu" is "I look". Max says "I look" for "I\'m watching", with "vnimatel\'no", "carefully", in the middle.'),
    (5, None, '\'Exactly three minutes remain until the dish is ready.\' "Ostalos\'" is "remained": Russian says "there remained three minutes". After "tri", three, "minuta" becomes "minuty", while after seven it lost its ending completely: "minut".'),
    (6, None, '\'Keep close watch on the fire on the skillet.\' "Prosledi za", "keep watch on", uses the same "za" as "sledi za poryadkom" at the cutting board. "Ogon\'" is fire, and "na skovorode" is "on the pan".'),
    (7, None, '\'Yes, Chef, I am not stepping away anywhere!\' "Ne otkhozhu nikuda" is a double negative: "not... nowhere". "Nikuda" is built like "nikogda", "never", and both begin with "ni-".'),
    (8, None, '\'The main thing is do not overcook this tender fillet.\' "Perezhar\'" is "pere-", over, plus "zharit\'", to fry: over-fry. "Glavnoye" is "the main thing", made from "glavnyy", main.'),
    (9, None, '\'I will remove the pan from the hot stove on time.\' "Vovremya", "on time", is "v", in, plus "vremya", time, written as one word. "S goryachey plity" is "off the hot stove": after "s" meaning "off", "plita" becomes "plity".'),
